WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.620
Hi, I'm very good.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.273
My name's Bradley Gordon,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.310
and I'm an advisor to the
the Cambodian government

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.230
to the Ministry of Culture.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.070
I understand that back in '94,
your company had an auction

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.550
of Khmer antiquities, and
we're just trying to figure

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.680
out where those pieces ended up.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.110
One of them is extremely
important to Cambodia

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.640
and it was looted from a temple

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.093
and we've collected a lot
of testimony about it.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.420
What we've been doing the
last two weeks, three weeks,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.950
we've been going around the States

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.570
and we've been visiting museums.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.419
We went to about 12 museums.

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:47.250
We went to Asian Art Museum
and Museum of Fine Arts

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.450
in Boston and Brooklyn
Art Museum and so on.

00:00:51.252 --> 00:00:52.085
Okay.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:55.653
Okay.

00:00:55.653 --> 00:00:56.486
I mean, totally.

00:00:56.486 --> 00:00:58.620
I mean, super appreciate that.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:00:59.493
That's fantastic.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.640
Okay. Thank you very much.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.510
Okay. Okay.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.117
Take care.

00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:07.530
As far as what, like confiscating it

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.593
or buying it from us or?

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.130
We don't buy stolen statues.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.710
So it's basically confiscating it.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.120
Um.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.170
You probably don't wanna use that word,

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.733
but basically that's the word. Right?

00:01:22.861 --> 00:01:26.202
Look, one of the dealers
who probably brought,

00:01:26.202 --> 00:01:28.353
brought many of these to the auction,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:35.090
he died about two years ago.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.413
Who was that?

00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:37.984
Douglas Latchford.

00:01:37.984 --> 00:01:38.817
I don't know if you've heard of him.

00:01:50.635 --> 00:01:55.635
The statues need to go back
home because the statues

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.940
symbolize Cambodian ancestors,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.188
Cambodian gods.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:04.817
Every time you go to temple, we expect to

00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:07.941
see the god over there, to
see our ancestor over there.

00:02:07.941 --> 00:02:10.117
But when we go to the temple,

00:02:10.117 --> 00:02:11.820
we didn't see anything over there.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.580
People chop it out and then cut the neck,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.220
cut the legs, and then move away from,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.220
from the temple and then to
the broker and to the dealer

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.523
and to collectors.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.650
This country was racked by
civil war for a 30 year period,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.470
from the late 60s to the late 90s.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.563
Millions died, millions
were made refugees.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.150
And in that time this
cultural heritage was stolen

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.503
at huge scale.

00:02:46.792 --> 00:02:48.420
The temples are some

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.080
of the most spectacular ancient
sites you can encounter.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.720
The one most people are
familiar with is Angkor.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.000
I believe it's right
to call it the largest

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.770
religious complex in the world.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.210
It is absolutely enormous.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.030
These very large statues
they're, you know,

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.860
four and five feet tall, some of them.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.030
They're incredibly beautiful.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:09.360
They're the product,
obviously, of immense skill

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.393
and a lot of really
impressive artisanship.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.930
Most people, however,
would only have seen them

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.020
in museums because the
statues are all gone

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.150
and that's because they were all stolen.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.240
The Denver Art Museum is now
returning four artifacts back

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.540
to Cambodia after they were
linked to a man who was indicted

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.310
by prosecutors two years ago.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.140
Douglas Latchford was indicted

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.633
after allegedly trafficking
stolen artifacts.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.340
Douglas Latchford was
a British businessmen,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.520
who lived really his entire
adult life in Bangkok,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.320
who made a lot of money

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.800
in pharmaceuticals and
real estate and was also

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.820
the world's most prominent, and arguably,

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.800
most prolific dealer of Cambodian statues.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.090
Douglas was known derisively
among heritage activists

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.860
as Dynamite Doug.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.190
And that was for the
rumored means by which

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.713
some of his objects were
extracted from temples.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.140
Douglas used this term for
himself, describing himself

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.120
as an adventurer scholar,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.430
which is is obviously calculated

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.670
to create a kind of Indiana Jones vibe.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.470
That was not really him.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.940
But in fact, Douglas was a
guy who liked five star hotels

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.000
and bespoke suits and nice restaurants.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.130
This is not someone who was ever trooping

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.460
around the jungle in
search of lost treasures.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.020
He got involved with something called

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.320
the Thai Bodybuilding and
Physique Sports Association

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.440
which is the official
national organization

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.570
for bodybuilders in Thailand.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.230
And Douglas, who, by this
point, was quite wealthy,

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.450
bankrolled the association.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.350
He ultimately served as the president.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.150
He was a businessman and he did most

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.950
of his business from the
comfort of his home in Bangkok.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.830
Items that, in one way or another,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.350
passed through Latchford's hands ended up

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.930
in the collections of
some very wealthy people,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.690
ended up in the Metropolitan
Museum in New York,

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.930
in the Norton Simon in
Pasadena, California,

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.020
in the British Museum, the
National Gallery of Australia.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.680
We don't actually know how
many pieces he bought and sold.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.280
Certainly in the hundreds,
perhaps in the thousands.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.101
We went into The Met and we took 3D images

00:05:38.101 --> 00:05:43.101
of every Cambodian piece that's on display

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.620
and fully documented what is here.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.620
We will be bringing that
back home and discussing

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.514
with the team back in
Phnom Penh to analyze

00:05:52.514 --> 00:05:56.010
and closely scrutinize, you know,

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:57.693
the details of these objects.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.700
There's been a big evolution in thinking

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.853
around cultural heritage
and who it belongs to.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:40.620
In 1970, there was a convention
that was put together

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:41.850
through UNESCO, which is the

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.550
the UN body for cultural heritage,

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:49.550
that laid down some ground
rules for how statues,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.660
you know, paintings, cultural objects,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.960
essentially, can be removed
from their country of origin.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.976
And the basic principle is, post 1970,

00:06:58.976 --> 00:07:00.810
you are supposed to show

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.330
that you have permission
from the government

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.720
of the country where an object
originated to remove it.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.210
There's no indication that
that Latchford had permission.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.810
The Cambodian government
certainly never granted permission

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.210
for the removal of any of these objects.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.590
Today, I am proud to announce

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.440
that we are returning
30 ancient works of art

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.510
to the Kingdom of Cambodia.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.600
Organized looting networks,
including looters affiliated

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.660
with the Khmer Rouge, sent these statues

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.700
to a well known antiquities dealer,

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.167
Douglas Latchford.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.500
The looters would go
get what they could find

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.670
and they would bring it

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.980
to the brokers who would
get it into Thailand.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.630
And then ultimately it would
find its way to Douglas.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:47.280
So there is a large team
of researchers centered

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.490
on an American lawyer named
Bradley Gordon who lives

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:54.059
in Cambodia, who are now trying to track

00:07:54.059 --> 00:07:57.723
down objects that were
looted from Cambodia.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.780
This team are interviewing
these looters in great detail,

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.230
going through step by step,
everything they remember.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.470
They will have Cambodian
government archeologists dig

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.350
up a temple and look for fragments,

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.223
look for broken feet
or pieces of pedestals.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.670
You can 3D match these fragments

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.490
to what is known to be
in museum collections,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.713
or the collections of private collectors.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:30.240
If that works, you've found a crime scene,

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.440
and that, as you can imagine,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.160
provides a basis to demand
that these pieces be given back

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:36.760
because they're stolen property.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.390
When I started, I was hired by

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.820
the US Department of Justice.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.400
I went out with Cambodians and we went

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.779
around the countryside
and we met maybe 100,

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:51.960
200 witnesses and we spoke

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.600
to them and we found one
gentleman in particular

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:55.893
we call him The Lion.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.400
He moved up to become the head

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.510
of a major gang and he had more than 300,

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.440
400 people working for
him across the country.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.273
And he told me

00:09:08.273 --> 00:09:11.070
that the one thing that kept
him going was the demand

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:12.330
that people in Bangkok,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.150
and one person in
particular named Sia Ford,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:20.150
who was Latchford, was eager
to get Khmer antiquities.

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.730
When I met The Lion, I brought one

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.650
of the books that Latchford had published

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.230
and he got very excited
when he saw the front cover

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.360
he saw Shiva and Skanda,

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.870
a father and child statue,
extremely important statue.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.367
And he was excited and saying to his wife

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:39.090
"I know this statue, I know this statue."

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.490
And later he showed me
the spot where he found it

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:49.503
and we dug and we found the
arm and we found the ear.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:55.340
I think looters, now, they want to help us

00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:00.300
because they feel more
likely, they feel guilty.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:05.160
They feel so sad when
everything just gone.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.030
They know also that those
antiquities gone from Cambodia

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.850
and it's results from their activity,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.473
from their mistake, also.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.830
Today I'm at the stone
laboratory of the National Museum

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.390
in Phnom Penh.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:29.313
And behind me is Shiva
with a statue of Skanda.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:34.020
We brought one of Lion's
lieutenants to this very room

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.690
a couple months ago and
he saw Shiva and Skanda,

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:37.620
and he touched it.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.663
And he said, "I believe you now.

00:10:40.867 --> 00:10:42.817
"I understand what you're doing.

00:10:42.817 --> 00:10:45.690
"And I see the results."

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.450
And they continue to help us.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.990
From about 2016, when a
very prominent New York

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:55.650
antiquities dealer named Nancy
Wiener was charged criminally

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.360
with possession of stolen goods,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.120
Douglas knew that he
was in the crosshairs,

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.970
and he began working
to try and head it off

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.090
through these negotiations
with the Cambodian government.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.010
And his ask of the Cambodian government

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.890
was that in exchange for me
giving back my collection

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:13.623
I want you to ask the
Americans not to prosecute.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:19.170
One day, a couple years ago, my phone rang

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.090
and I answered the phone
and it was Douglas.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.210
He had instructions not to
talk to me at that stage.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:28.260
So he called me and he said,
I'm back from the dead.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:31.860
And he started laughing
and we started talking.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:35.370
He was an extremely charming
man, and a very good salesman.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.230
And there's no question about that.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:39.060
And I said to him, look, Douglas,

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.880
we need your collection to come home.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.820
And if you truly love
the Cambodian people,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:46.530
this would be the right thing to do.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:47.730
And he laughed.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:52.730
And he said, "Yes, I would
like to give back to Cambodia."

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:58.470
I now have access to a
lot of his correspondence.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.690
And I see that, sadly,

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.330
he was actually selling his collection.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.670
He was trying to get rid of it

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.620
and putting it in other people's hands.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:13.650
You know, he was extremely busy trying to

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.993
move his collection
right up until the end.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.290
It's kind of sad, you
know, for us as Cambodians,

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:29.400
who have a very long, very great history,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.880
but now we seem to lose it.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.780
We become someone who have nothing.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.610
The antiquities [are] important because

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.170
they are not the statue,

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.510
they are not the object,

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:46.260
they are belong to, and our ancestors.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.707
So that is important that the statues,

00:12:48.707 --> 00:12:50.032
that the antiquities, came back,

00:12:50.032 --> 00:12:52.020
our ancestors came back to us,

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:56.493
and then we can continue
our practice, our worship.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.770
In many ways, our work
is just getting started.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:05.790
We're tracking more than
100 museums with more

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.620
than 2000 objects.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.690
Now there are some major
collections out there.

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:14.010
So we're taking a strong line
when we're talking to museums

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.750
and we're talking to private
collectors, we're saying,

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.430
prove to us you have a right to have it.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:18.263
It's stolen.

00:13:18.263 --> 00:13:19.680
We know it's stolen.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.170
Some of the museums are
being very transparent

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.500
and forthcoming and
friendly about the situation

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.080
and open to the discussion.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:29.490
Others are stonewalling.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.440
They don't want to talk to us.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.830
The US government has
contacted private collectors

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.470
and they've had a lot
of incredible successes.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.450
You know, we had Jim Clark
returning a collection

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.390
that he paid more than $35 million for.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.900
Returning this artifact is
like returning our pride,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.750
returning our soul back to our peoples.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.730
It's been very frustrating

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:54.000
because we're doing all
this work here in Cambodia,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.150
in a country that is still developing

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.220
without full cooperation

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:04.220
from extremely sophisticated
and wealthy institutions

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.690
and without the help
of private collectors,

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.340
many of them are billionaires.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:11.220
These individuals, a hundred
of them, I know their names.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:12.660
I have their email addresses.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:13.590
They're out there.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.690
They are completely silent.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.780
None of them are coming forward

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:20.520
and providing us information.

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.734
If we had all that information,

00:14:22.734 --> 00:14:26.400
I think we could prove to
almost every museum in the world

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.470
most of their collection is stolen.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:29.643
It needs to come home.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:36.540
So when I walk into Met museum

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.150
and I saw this female
statue, Kok Ker style.

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:42.462
So I feel very, very proud,

00:14:42.462 --> 00:14:45.161
feeling like [we] miss each other,

00:14:45.161 --> 00:14:47.760
like my heart, like show up.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.276
I love this statue so much.

00:14:50.276 --> 00:14:52.890
When I see it, I never think about

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.687
I have chance to see this statue.

00:14:55.687 --> 00:14:56.670
Do you feel sad?

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.143
Yes. Very upset.

